The U.S. state of [[Maine]] first required its residents to register their [[motor vehicle]]s and display [[Vehicle registration plate|license plate]]s in 1905. {{As of|2022}}, plates are issued by the Maine Bureau of Motor Vehicles, within the office of the [[Secretary of State of Maine|Department of the Secretary of State]]. Front and rear plates are required for most classes of vehicles, while only rear plates are required for motorcycles and trailers.

Maine legislators effectively removed a potentially unconstitutional review process for [[vanity plate]]s in 2015, which formerly was able to reject plates that were "obscene, contemptuous, profane or prejudicial."<ref>{{cite news |last1=Sharp |first1=David |title=Maine close to ridding license plates of obscenities |url=https://www.boston.com/news/local-news/2022/05/05/maine-close-to-ridding-license-plates-of-obscenities/ |work=Boston.com |agency=Associated Press |date=May 6, 2022}}</ref> Due to a proliferation of vulgar plates over the following years, the legislature directed the [[Maine Bureau of Motor Vehicles|Bureau of Motor Vehicles]] to establish new rules for reviewing plates in 2021. The new rules ban profane or obscene language, language inciting violence, and derogatory references "to age, race, ethnicity, sexual orientation, gender identity, national origin, religion or disability." A committee for vanity plates consults the online [[Urban Dictionary]] for assistance with "arcane lingo".<ref>{{cite news |last1=Calvert |first1=Scott |url=https://www.wsj.com/articles/maine-obscene-license-plates-crack-down-11655301439 |title=What the BLEEP? Maine Is Cracking Down on Obscene License Plates |newspaper=The Wall Street Journal |date=June 15, 2022 }}</ref>

